  "worked_solution": "To determine whether the line $y=x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x}-k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-k &  \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-k & =\\frac{k}{x}-k \\\\ x-k & =\\frac{k-kx}{x} \\\\ x(x-k) & =k-kx \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola at two points, the quadratic formula above must give two real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be positive: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k\\in (0,\\infty ).$",
